<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Philippe I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Louis-Philippe of Orléans, Louis Philippe, Duke of Orléans, Louis-Philippe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Philippe I was King of the French from 1830 to 1848. Head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> and son of a prince who had backed the Revolution, he was placed on the throne after the July Revolution of 1830 as a constitutional 'citizen king'. His July Monarchy favoured the wealthy middle class, and he was overthrown in the Revolution of 1848 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i.jpg" alt="Louis Philippe I" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Xaver Winterhalter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1841_portrait_painting_of_Louis_Philippe_I_(King_of_the_French)_by_Winterhalter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
